    "Surveillance after thoracic aneurysm repair involves using imaging techniques to monitor for complications or residual aortic pathology. CT is generally preferred post-TEVAR, whereas MRI is a viable alternative, especially for reducing radiation exposure. Longitudinal surveillance using MRI is recommended to avoid radiation or iodinated contrast allergies. Open repair offers durable solutions for thoracic aortas, but has a higher reintervention rate in cases of complications such as endoleaks and aortic dissection. Regular imaging surveillance is advised for patients after both TEVAR and open repair. Guidelines recommend different surveillance intervals based on imaging results and repair type.": [],
